US-032 (render graph) is implemented and every gate that can run here passes. The Windows and Linux quality gates, the GCC job, the ASan/UBSan sanitize run, and the real-GPU suites on the RTX 5080 all pass. The only Linux test failure is the known X11 input flake, and it did not recur on the rerun.

**What was built** (new library `engine_render` in `engine/render/`, the path the PRD assigns):
- **Declaration:** passes list their resource accesses (`ColorWrite`, `ShaderRead`, `CopySource`, …) and a kind (graphics or compute). The access selects the state, so passes never name states directly.
- **Compile:** `RenderGraph::compile()` orders passes with a stable topological sort and derives per-resource barriers. It rejects cycles, read-before-write, multiple writers, duplicate names, and incompatible access/kind/usage. Each error names the affected passes. Transients with the same layout and disjoint lifetimes share one allocation.
- **Execute:** `execute()` records one graphics command list with GPU timestamps and CPU timers, submits once, waits, and releases transients. A failing pass is named in the error, and nothing leaks.
- **Export:** `export_json()` writes passes, barriers, edges, lifetimes, and timings.

**Results:**
- **Linux:** `linux-unit` 22/22 (the CPU suite is 414 cases, 15 of them new). GCC unit 22/22. `linux-asan` builds with zero warnings, and `linux-sanitize` 22/22 on the rerun. The first ASan run had one failure, the known X11 input case at `platform_x11_input_tests.cpp:437`.
- **Windows:** `ValidateVendor`, `win-dev`, and `win-debug` pass with zero `warning C` lines. `win-unit` is 21/21. On the RTX 5080 with debug layers on, `--suite gpu --backend dx12` passes 15 cases and `--suite gpu --backend vulkan` passes 16. The new GPU case runs the same clear → copy → download chain on both backends, with a reused allocation, repeated three times with stable live counts.
- **Not run:** GitHub CI (not pushed), the Linux native GPU suites (no usable Vulkan device on this host), `engine_bench --suite acceptance` (no bench target exists yet), and any GUI check (no editor yet).

**Choices I made that you may want to redirect:**
- **Compute passes run on the graphics queue.** Vulkan textures use exclusive sharing, so overlapping queues would need ownership transfers that the RHI does not record. Async compute is left for a later story.
- **`execute()` is synchronous.** It waits on the frame. Frame pipelining belongs to the runtime loop in US-033 onward.
- **Each resource has at most one writer per frame.** This keeps ordering derived from data, and it is what makes cycles detectable.

